"Which pathways cover the most unique biomarkers for patients with disorders related to purine, pyrimidine, and urea cycle metabolism?","The top pathways covering the most unique biomarkers are 'Pyrimidine metabolism and related diseases' (WP4225), 'Amino acid metabolism' (WP3925), and 'Purine metabolism' (WP4792). For example, for the UPB1D patient, these pathways cover 5, 3, and 3 unique biomarkers respectively. Similarly, for the DPYD patient, 'Amino acid metabolism' (WP3925) covers 6 biomarkers, 'Biomarkers for pyrimidine metabolism disorders' (WP4584) covers 5, and 'Urea cycle and related diseases' (WP4571) covers 1 unique biomarker.",2022.01.31.21265847.full.pdf,24,11,"license It is made available under a perpetuity. is the author/funder, who has granted medRxiv a license to display the preprint in (which was not certified by peer review)preprint The copyright holder for this this version posted February 4, 2022. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.01.31.21265847doi: medRxiv preprint well as how many unique biomarkers the highest-scoring pathway covers. Table 6 shows which pathways ended up in the top three for each patient, as well as which biomarkers were not part of the visualization after selecting the top three. Figure 2: Visualization of how theoretical biochemical markers in urine overlap for individual IMDs in the purine, pyrimidine, and urea cycle pathways. Protein names correspond to the following disorders: PRPS1: Phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate synthetase 1 superactivity, XAN2: Xanthinuria, Type II, ADSL: Adenyl- succinate lyase deficiency, ATIC: AICAR transformylase/IMP cyclohydrolase deficiency, APRT: Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase deficiency, HPRT1: Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, ADA: Adenosine deaminase deficiency, HPRT1_less: Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome, PNP: Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency, XO: Xanthinuria, Type I, DGUOK: Deoxyguanosine kinase deficiency, NT5C3A: Pyrimidine 5-nucleotidase superactivity, DPYD: Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase defic