"What methods were used to differentiate gut microbial and metabolic features among ATB, LTBI, and healthy controls in the study?","The study used amplicon sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene and untargeted metabolomics approaches, combined with statistical analyses, to explore gut microbial and metabolic differences among patients with active tuberculosis (ATB), latent tuberculosis infection (LTBI), and healthy controls (HCs).",msystems.00812-24.pdf,28,2,"investigated the gut microbiota in individuals with LTBI. In addition, the metabolic alterations in the gut microbiota caused by compositional dysbiosis remain poorly defined in patients with TB. In this study, we sought to explore the gut microbial and metabolic differences among patients with ATB, LTBI, and healthy controls (HCs) using amplicon sequencing and untargeted metabolomics approaches. Through appropriate statistical analyses, we ultimately detected early functional alterations in the gut microbiota that arise after M. tb infection and metabolic features that can effectively discriminate between individuals with LTBI and HCs. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study design The design and execution of the present study are summarized and simplified in a flowchart (Fig. 1A ). We conducted this study in Pingnan County (TB case notification rate of 58.48 cases per 100,000 population per year) in the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region of China. This study included 33 incident cases of ATB, 30 individuals with LTBI, and 30 HCs from Pingnan People’s Hospital, the exclusive designated medical institution for TB prevention and treatment in Pingnan. Patients with ATB and LTBI were diagnosed in accordance with the Technical Guidelines for Tuberculosis Prevention and Control in China issued by the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention in 2021. Patients with ATB were recruited from the Department of TB Prevention and Treatment and were defined as follows: (i) clinical symptoms and chest imaging evidence; (ii) conclusive evidence of M. tb from at least one method including culture, GeneXpert MTB/RIF, and/or next-generation metagenome sequencing (mNGS) of sputum or bronchoalveolar lavage fluid samples; and (iii) no evidence of extrapulmonary TB. Individuals with LTBI and HCs were recruited from the physical examination center of Pingnan People’s Hospital. LTBI was defined by positive results from both the tuberculin skin test (0.1 mL dose of purified protein derivative, Chengdu, China; >10",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
What are the clinical features and associated metabolic abnormalities of inherited myoadenylate deaminase deficiency?,"Inherited myoadenylate deaminase deficiency presents with exercise intolerance, myalgia, and cramping, and is typically not associated with metabolic abnormalities. It can be asymptomatic, show functional abnormalities in physiological studies, or be symptomatic with exertional myalgias that are usually nonprogressive. Symptoms may involve other factors, and coincidental heterogeneous symptoms depend on associated disorders, potentially synergistic if metabolic.",nihms635383.pdf,29,14,"mitochondrialdGK Psychomotor retardation Multiorgan system failure DNA depletion syndromes Hereditary xanthinuria XOR None Hypouricemia, nephrolithiasis Lesch–Nyhan disease HPRT Motor disability, self-injurious behavior, intellectual disabilityHyperuricemia, nephrolithiasis, gout, tophi Myoadenylate deaminase deficiencymAMPD Exercise intolerance, myalgia, crampingNone Phosphoribosylpyrophosphate synthase superactivityPRPP synthaseDeafness (± psychomotor retardation)Hyperuricemia, nephrolithiasis, gout Phosphoribosylpyrophosphate synthase deficiency: Charcot– Marie–Tooth CMTX5, or Arts syndromePRPP synthaseDeafness, neuropathy, optic neuropathy (± psychomotor retardation)None Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiencyPNP Uncommon Immunological defects Renal hypouricemia URAT1 None Hypouricemia, nephrolithiasis, renal failure Retinitis pigmentosa type 10, or Leber congenital amaurosisIDH-1 Retinal degeneration None Severe combined ADA Variable Life-threatening infections immunodeficiency syndrome (SCID) *See legend to Figure 187.1 for key to the abbreviations used in the table. Handb Clin Neurol . Author manuscript; available in PMC 2014 October 31. NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author ManuscriptJinnah et al. Page 18 Table 187.2 Variable phenotypes of inherited myoadenylate deaminase deficiency Classification Clinical features Comments Asymptomatic None 1 Functional abnormalities noted in physiological studies 2 Affected individuals may be at risk of developing symptoms Symptomatic Exertional myalgias 1 Typically nonprogressive 2 Proposed to involve other factors Coincidental Heterogeneous 1 Nature and severity dependent on associated disorder 2 May be synergistic if associated disorder is metabolic Handb Clin Neurol . Author manuscript; available in PMC 2014 October 31.",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
