What are the main clinical features and genetic characteristics of adenylosuccinate lyase (ADSL) deficiency?,"ADSL deficiency presents with diverse clinical features including psychomotor retardation from birth, epilepsy in infancy, autistic behaviors, hypotonia, feeding problems, growth retardation, and occasional microcephaly. Type I patients show severe symptoms, while type II patients have milder intellectual impairment and sometimes autism with few seizures. The disorder is inherited autosomal recessively, caused by over 50 mutations in the ADSL gene on chromosome 22q, mostly missense mutations leading to enzyme instability and reduced activity. Severity correlates with residual enzyme activity. Common mutations include R426H and R303C, the latter associated with milder symptoms and altered substrate activity ratios.",nihms635383.pdf,29,1,"ADENYLOSUCCINATELYASE DEFICIENCY Clinical features The clinical presentations of adenylosuccinate lyase (ADSL) deficiency are very diverse. Most of the initial reported patients, often referred to as type I, display variable associations of psychomotor retardation from birth, followed by epilepsy in infancy, autistic features (absence of eye contact, repetitive gestures, bouts of agitation, temper tantrums, self- injurious behavior) hypotonia, feeding problems, and occasional growth retardation with muscle wasting ( Van den Berge and Jaeken, 2001 ). Later, patients were diagnosed who presented with intractable neonatal convulsions. A very early presentation has been described with intrauterine growth impairment, microcephaly, fetal hypokinesia, and absence of fetal heart rate variability. This was followed after birth by severe hypotonia requiring mechanical ventilation, intractable seizures, and early death ( Mouchegh et al., 2007 ). Microcephaly is often present. As a rule there are no other dysmorphic features, except for two patients with brachycephaly associated with a long, flat philtrum and thin upper lip ( Holder-Espinasse et al., 2002 ). Rare patients, referred to as type II, are strikingly less intellectually impaired as compared to type I. One patient displayed only delayed motor development and profound hypotonia. Another boy presented with high functioning autism without other symptoms except for two epileptic seizures ( Stathis et al., 2000 ). Recently ADSL deficiency was diagnosed in two sisters with a behavioral phenotype resembling Angelman syndrome ( Gitiaux et al., 2009 ). Genetics ADSL deficiency is inherited as an autosomal recessive trait. To date, the defect has been diagnosed in over 70 patients in various European countries and in the USA. The disorder seems to be rare in the UK. More than 50 mutations of the ADSL gene, located on chromosome 22q, have been identified. Most are missense mutations, and about half of the patients are compound heterozygotes. A common mutation",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
What mass spectrometry methods were used for qualitative and quantitative analysis of tryptic peptides in the study?,"Qualitative analysis was performed using a data-dependent acquisition (DDA) workflow, while quantitative analysis was conducted using sequential window acquisition of all theoretical fragment ion spectra (SWATH-MS), a data-independent acquisition (DIA) method, both utilizing a TripleTOF 6600 LC-MS/MS system.",1-s2.0-S1931524424000355-main.pdf,30,5,"grade). The gel was reduced in 10 mM dithiothreitol (Sigma-Aldrich) in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (Sig- ma-Aldrich) at 56•C for 30 min and alkylated with 55 mM iodoaceta - mide (Sigma-Aldrich) in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate for 20 min at room temperature in darkness. Subsequently, the gel sections were rinsed with 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate in 50 % methanol and dehydrated with acetonitrile. Tryptic digestion was performed by add- ing modified porcine trypsin (Promega) at a final concentration of 20 ng/µL in 20 mM ammonium bicarbonate for 16 h at 37•C. To extract the Fig. 1.Workflow of the overall clinical study . α-GalA, α-galactosidase A; CTL, control; DDA, data-dependent acquisition; FD, Fabry disease; LC-MS/MS, liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry; lyso-GB3, globotriaosylsphingosine; SWATH, sequential window acquisition of all theorical fragment ion spectra. L. L˘opez-Valverde et al. Translational Research 269 (2024) 47–63 50tryptic peptides generated, three 20-min incubations were performed in 40 µL 60 % acetonitrile and 0.5 % formic acid, and the samples pooled and vacuum dried in a SpeedVac for storage at -20•C for further analyses. Digested peptides (F4 µg of each sample) were separated using reverse phase chromatography. The gradient was created using a micro liquid chromatography system (Eksigent Technologies nanoLC 400, Sciex) coupled to a high-speed Triple TOF 6600 mass spectrometer (Sciex, Foster City, C) with a microflow source, as described previ - ously .32 Qualitive analysis was performed using a data-dependent workflow (DDA) as described previously .32 Further details can be found in the Supplementary Material and Methods. Quantitative analysis was performed by sequential window acqui - sition of all theoretical fragment ion spectra (SWATH-MS). To build the mass spectrometry (MS/MS) spectral libraries, pools of peptide solutions per condition were analysed using a shotgun data-dependent acquisition (DDA) approach by micro-LC-MS/MS (Sciex, USA), as described above and previously.32,35,36 After",True,1,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
