ents with Fabry disease - A systematic literature review by a European panel of experts. Mol. Genet. Metab. 126, 224–235 (2019). 14. Feldt-Rasmussen, U. et al. Long-term efficacy and safety of migalastat",True,2,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
How is serum neurofilament light-chain (sNfL) concentration related to Fabry Disease (FD) severity and neurological manifestations?,"In people with Fabry Disease (PwFD), serum neurofilament light-chain (sNfL) concentrations are significantly higher compared to healthy controls, with males showing higher sNfL levels than females. sNfL Z-scores positively correlate with disease severity measured by FOS-MSSI. Additionally, PwFD with ischemic white matter lesions on MRI have significantly elevated sNfL values, indicating sNfL as a potential biomarker for neurological involvement and disease burden in FD.",s41598-024-73537-y.pdf,37,1,"small-fiber neuropathy7. Additionally, studies hinted on FD-patients being increasingly affected by microstructural white matter changes, neurodegenerative changes, as well as cognitive dysfunction and depression8–10. After establishing the diagnosis, two disease-specific forms of therapy are available: (i) three different formulations of intravenous enzyme replacement therapy (ERT) and (ii) an oral chaperone therapy. Both have shown efficacy in reducing disease burden and improving quality of life, as well as prevention of disease progression with stabilization of organ dysfunction and enhancement of lifespan11–15. 1Department of Neurology, Medical University of Vienna, Währinger Gürtel 18-20, 1090 Vienna, Austria. 2Comprehensive Center for Clinical Neurosciences and Mental Health, Medical University of Vienna, Vienna, Austria. 3Division of Cardiology, Department of Medicine II, Medical University of Vienna, Vienna, Austria. 4Division of Nephrology and Dialysis, Department of Medicine III, Medical University of Vienna, Vienna, Austria. email: markus.ponleitner@meduniwien.ac.at OPEN Scientific Reports | (2024) 14:23033 1 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-73537-y www.nature.com/scientificreports In PwFD, regular follow-up appointments including blood tests, imaging, ultrasound and clinical examinations are required to guarantee standard of care in the outpatient setting22. Neurofilament light-chain (NfL), an intermediary filament predominately expressed in myelinated axons and responsible for axonal diameter16,17, has been established as a valuable diagnostic and prognostic tool in many neurological diseases in recent years18. The advent of fourth-generation detection assays now allows reliable detection of NfL in peripheral blood (serum or plasma), thus markedly facilitating sample acquisition. Large studies have looked at the distribution of NfL-concentrations in healthy individuals and suggested reference values partitioned for age and body mass index, an important milestone in establishing this biomarker for routine patient care19,20. Additionally, standardized Z-scores adjusted for age and body mass index have been published21. This study aimed to investigate the possible value of adding the biomarker sNfL to the diagnostic and monitoring armamentarium in the assessment of neurological disease manifestations",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
"What analytical method was developed for the identification and quantification of Gb3 and Lyso-Gb3 in Fabry disease patients, and what are its advantages?","A liquid chromatography coupled to multiple reaction monitoring mass spectrometry (LC-MRM/MS) method was developed for the identification and quantification of Gb3 and Lyso-Gb3 in serum and blood samples of Fabry disease patients. The method uses glycolipid extraction with chloroform/methanol/water and separation on a C4 column with a specific gradient. It provides a reliable, fast, and effective procedure for direct measurement of Gb3 and Lyso-Gb3, serving as a complementary assay to current enzymatic tests and potentially improving clinical diagnostics of lysosomal storage disorders.",separations-11-00239.pdf,38,0,"Citation: Battaglia, G.; Pinto, G.; Fontanarosa, C.; Spinelli, M.; Illiano, A.; Serpico, S.; Chiariotti, L.; Risoluti, R.; Materazzi, S.; Amoresano, A. Determination of Gb3 and Lyso-Gb3 in Fabry Disease-Affected Patients by LC-MRM/MS. Separations 2024 ,11, 239. https://doi.org/10.3390/ separations11080239 Academic Editor: Sylwia Studzi ´ nska Received: 6 July 2024 Revised: 25 July 2024 Accepted: 31 July 2024 Published: 6 August 2024 Copyright: ©2024 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license (https:// creativecommons.org/licenses/by/ 4.0/). separations Article Determination of Gb3 and Lyso-Gb3 in Fabry Disease-Affected Patients by LC-MRM/MS Gennaro Battaglia1 , Gabriella Pinto1,2,* , Carolina Fontanarosa1, Michele Spinelli1 , Anna Illiano1 , Stefania Serpico1 , Lorenzo Chiariotti3, Roberta Risoluti4 , Stefano Materazzi4 and Angela Amoresano1,2 1Department of Chemical Sciences, University of Naples Federico II, Via Cinthia 26, 80126 Naples, Italy; gennaro.battaglia@unina.it (G.B.); carolina.fontanarosa@unina.it (C.F.); michele.spinelli@unina.it (M.S.); stefania.serpico@uniroma1.it (S.S.); angela.amoresano@unina.it (A.A.) 2National Institute of Biostructures and Biosystems (INBB)—Inter-University Consortium, Viale delle Medaglie d‘Oro, 305, 00136 Rome, Italy 3Department of Molecular Medicine and Medical Biotechnologies, School of Medicine, University of Naples Federico II, Via Domenico Montesano, 49, 80131 Naples, Italy; lorenzo.chiariotti@unina.it 4Department of Chemistry, Sapienza University of Rome, p.le A. Moro 5, 00185 Rome, Italy; roberta.risoluti@uniroma1.it (R.R.); stefano.materazzi@uniroma1.it (S.M.) *Correspondence: gabriella.pinto@unina.it Abstract: Limited or absent activity of the enzyme α-galactosidase A ( α-Gal A), due to mutation in the related gene on the X chromosome, leads to the development of a rare hereditary and genetic disease known as Fabry disease (FD). This pathology involves a progressive accumulation in various organs of the substrates of the enzyme e.g., globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) and its deacylated form, globotriaosylsphingosine (Lyso-Gb3), suggesting these molecules as biomarkers of Fabry disease. The present paper describes the development of an analytical strategy for",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
