What factors contribute to early microalbuminuria and kidney complications in GSD-I patients according to the study?,"Early microalbuminuria in GSD-I patients may result from late diagnosis and poor metabolic control, as indicated by high alanine, lactic acid, and triglyceride levels. Hyperuricemia, caused by inhibition of uric acid excretion due to lactic acid and increased adenine nucleotide degradation, also contributes to kidney injury. Despite treatment with allopurinol, persistent hyperuricemia can occur. Additionally, hypocitraturia and tubular dysfunction may develop even with optimal metabolic control, possibly due to incomplete distal tubular acidosis. Kidney stone formation is linked to chronic acidosis, hypocitraturia, hypercalciuria, and hyperuricemia. Hypertension appears not to be a major factor in glomerular injury in most cases, and no correlation was found between BMI and microalbuminuria in GSD-I patients.",fped-08-00591.pdf,59,7,"other reports in the literature ( 9). In contrast to a study by Martens et al. ( 17), early appearance of microalbuminuria in our patients may have been due to the late diagnosis of GSD and/or poor metabolic control, as reﬂected by high alanine, lactic acid, and trigl yceride Frontiers in Pediatrics | www.frontiersin.org 5 September 2020 | Volume 8 | Article 591 Aoun et al. GSD-1: Kidney and Metabolic Phenotypes levels. Chen et al. ( 18), reported kidney failure in 40 percent of their cohort and one death due to kidney failure while in ourseries,onlyonepatientprogressedtochronickidneydise ase requiring dialysis at the age of 18 years. Patients who are we ll- controlled are more likely to have preserved renal function a nd late, if any, appearance of microalbuminuria. Control of uric acid is very important among the metabolic markers. Indeed, uric acid exerts a deleterious eﬀect on tubular cells by induci ng epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) in the proximal tubules. Hyperuricemia in GSD-I can be explained by a dual mechanism of inhibition of uric acid tubular excretion by lac tic acid along with an increased degradation of adenine nucleot ides (19). The treatment of hyperuricemia with allopurinol has been reported to avoid kidney injury ( 20). Persistent hyperuricemia was observed particularly in 2 patients with good metabolic control,despitetreatmentwithaxanthineoxidaseinhibito r(5). As reported by Weinstein et al. ( 10), optimal metabolic control did not seem to prevent hypocitraturia and tubular dysfunction: half of the patients developed hypocitraturia af ter a variable time of evolution, ranging between 21 months and 12 years. This might be possibly explained by the development of an incomplete distal tubular acidosis ( 9). Although the exact mechanism for kidney stone formation in GSD-1 is not clear, chronic acidosis, hypocitraturia, hypercalciuri a and hyperuricemia are thought to be contributing factors. Reista",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
"What factors contribute to the progression of kidney disease in GSD 1 patients, and how effective is metabolic control in preventing renal failure?","The progression of kidney disease in GSD 1 patients is associated with activation of pathways such as glycolysis, de novo lipogenesis, and the renin-angiotensin system, leading to silent glomerular hyperfiltration, microalbuminuria, proteinuria, and systemic arterial hypertension. Although kidney disease appears inevitable, the role of metabolic control is controversial because glucose 6 phosphate is absent in the glomeruli, and strict metabolic control mainly affects tubulopathy rather than glomerulopathy. Therefore, even with optimal metabolic control and ACE inhibitor therapy, some patients still progress to kidney failure.",fped-08-00591.pdf,59,4,"been described. Activation of various pathways, in particular, glycolysis, de novo lipogenesis, and activation of the renin angiotensin system m ay lead to the progression of kidney disease ( 7). Thus, kidney damage may develop after a period of “silent” glomerular hyperﬁltration,withmicroalbuminuria,proteinuriaandsyst emic arterialhypertension.Thesewillsubsequentlyleadtorena lfailure in a considerable number of patients ( 15). Although kidney disease seems inevitable in GSD 1 patients, the role of metaboli c control is controversial, since Glucose 6 phosphate is absent in the glomeruli and strict metabolic control can aﬀect usually the occurrence of tubulopathy more than glomerulopathy. Therefore, even with optimal metabolic control and ACEI therapy,somepatientswouldstillprogresstokidneyfailure( 12). Frontiers in Pediatrics | www.frontiersin.org 3 September 2020 | Volume 8 | Article 591 Aoun et al. GSD-1: Kidney and Metabolic PhenotypesTABLE 1 | Clinical presentation and kidney outcome of GSD type I patie nts. GSD Clinical features at diagnosis Growth and renal outcome at last follow up Gene test Patient/gender CG Age at Dg Sz D HG HtaWtaBMIaFU duration HtaWtaBMIaHypocitraturiabHypercalciuriacMicro- albuminuriadOutcome Ia 1/M No 3m +–+ −2.00 0.2 0.98 12 y −2 0.02 0.98 + – + GP 2/M Yes 4m + + + − 2.05−1.38−0.23 10m −1.2 0.21 1.26 – – – – 3/MaYes 5 y – +–−1.97−0.59 1.03 12 y 161* 67.5* 26* + + + GP, NL 4/M Yes 6m – – + −3.10−1.36 1.21 8 y −3.6−1.7 1.01 – – – – 5/F Yes 2 y 2m – – +2.50 2.32 1.12 10m 2.5 2.32 1.12 – – – – 6/F Yes 1 y 3m – +–−2.50−0.66 1.41 7 y 4m −2.7−0.2 1.55 + – – TD 7/M Yes 3 y – – – −1.40−1.04 2.07 1 y −1.15 1 2.7 – – – – 8/M Yes 6m – – – −2.20−1.04 1.12 4 y −1.9−0.56 1.48 – – + GP 9/M",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
What metabolic marker was identified as a promising indicator of metabolic control and potential early kidney disease risk in GSD-I patients?,"Plasma alanine was identified as a promising marker reflecting metabolic control and lactic acidemia, which may help in the follow-up of GSD-I patients and early identification of those at risk of kidney disease.",fped-08-00591.pdf,59,8,"To date, genotype-phenotype correlation studies do not show a strict correlation in GSD- Ia ( 26) nor in GSD-Ib ( 27,28). In our series, c.247C >T mutation of the G6P gene was detected in three GSD-Ia patients with glomerulopathy while 2 GSD-Ib with tubular dysfunction had c.1348G >A mutation and did not develop glomerulopathy after a follow-up period between 3 years 6 months and 10 years 8 months. However, we cannot draw any strong conclusion regarding phenotypic kidney outcome in correlation with a speciﬁc genotype in thisseries. It is known that the development of kidney disease in GSD- I patients might be delayed by good metabolic control in additi onto medical therapy with ACEI ( 16). In our study, in addition to blooduricacid,lacticacid,andtriglyceridelevels,weuse dplasma alanineasa markerofthemetabolic status.Chronic elevatio nof mean plasma alanine level was detected in all patients with poor metabolic control who developed microalbuminuria. To our knowledge, this is the only study where alanine was assessed to determine the metabolic status, in association with other k nown biochemical markers. We believe that the role of plasma alani ne as an early marker to identify metabolic disturbances should be determinedinfuturestudiesinGSD-Ipatients.Earlyelevati onof plasma alanine might help to identify patients at risk and henc e, toadapttheirdietand/ortreatment. CONCLUSION Plasma alanine, reﬂecting lactic acidemia, seems to be a promising marker, reﬂecting metabolic control, that may help in the follow-up of GSD-I patients. Further larger studies are still needed to identify the role of each biomarker in the progression of GSD-I kidney disease in order to optimize patient’s outcome and achieve therapeuticgoals. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT All datasets generated for this study are included in the article/supplementarymaterial. ETHICS STATEMENT The studies involving human participants were reviewed and approved by Institutional Review Board at the American University of Beirut. Written informed consent from the participants’ legal guardian/next",True,2,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
"What metabolic alterations are observed in GSDI patients despite adequate dietary treatment, and how do these relate to typical clinical markers and complications?","GSDI patients exhibit broad metabolic disturbances affecting fuel and energy metabolism, lipids and fatty acids, amino acid and methyl-group metabolism, urea cycle intermediates, and purine/pyrimidines, despite adequate dietary treatment. These alterations do not correlate with plasma triglycerides, lactate, or venous plasma glucose concentrations, nor with the presence of complications such as liver adenomas or microalbuminuria. Most metabolic changes occur in both GSD subtypes, with some differences in extent and a limited set of metabolites distinguishing the subtypes.",JIMD-45-235.pdf,60,8,"plasma triglyceride levels, nor the plasma glucose and lactate concentrations. Furthermore,the presence of liver adenomas was not associated with specific features/alterations in the fatty acid profile, such as higher levels of lipogenesis markers. However, therewas an increased proportion of palmitoleic acid, that is, the ratio 16:1/16:0 (0.185 vs 0.120, P=0.031) and a trend for higher total cholesterol levels (8.72 vs 6.36 mmol/L,P=0.097) in the subgroup of GSDIa patients with microalbuminuria. The proportion of di-homo- γ-linoleic Controls GSDIa GSDIb05101520 BiotinidaseActivity (nmol/min/ml)Controls GSDIa GSDIb0.00.51.01.52.0C20:3n6Relative abundance (%) Controls GSDIa GSDIb0.000.050.100.150.200.25Ratio C16:1/C16:0Molar Ratio Controls GSDIa GSDIb02468Ratio C18:1n9/C18:0Molar RatioControls GSDIa GSDIb0246810C16:1Relative abundance (%) Controls GSDIa GSDIb01020304050C18:1n9Relative abundance (%) Controls GSDIa GSDIb012345Ratio C16:0/C18:2n6Molar RatioControls GSDIa GSDIb01020304050C18:2n6Relative abundance (%) Controls GSDIa GSDIb1520253035 C16:0Relative abundance (%) Controls GSDIa GSDIb0510152025TriglyceridesConcentration (mmol/L) Controls GSDIa GSDIb051015CholesterolConcentration (mmol/L) FIGURE 4 Fatty aci