Can elevated PPCS levels in CDG patients be used as a reliable diagnostic marker for Niemann-Pick disease type C (NPC)?,"Elevated PPCS levels can be present in some CDG patients above the NPC diagnostic cut-off, but these elevations do not correlate with liver enzyme or bilirubin levels and are not specific to NPC. In contrast, TCG levels remain below the NPC diagnostic cut-off in all evaluated CDG samples, making TCG a more specific marker for NPC diagnosis.",nihms-1872629.pdf,67,4,"various CDG types. Table 1 contains information on demographics, genotype, liver enzyme, bilirubin, TCG, and PPCS levels. Additional clinical information and samples used in this work is in Supplemental Table 1. PPCS level above cut-off for diagnosing NPC1 was present in ATP6AP1-, ALG1-, ALG8-, and PMM2-CDG samples (Fig. 1). No CDG samples have TCG level above the diagnostic cut-off for NPC. Elevated PPCS levels do not correlate with levels of the liver enzymes ALT [Pearson correlation r(25) = −0.07, p = 0.71], AST [r(25) = −0.0002, p = 1], or alkaline phosphatase [r(25) = −0.11, p = 0.59] (Fig. 2A-C). PPCS levels (raw or log10 transformed) in samples do not differ significantly {non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis raw [ χ2(3) = 4.2, p = .24] or log10 transformed [ χ2(3) = 4.1, p = .25]} among group of samples with different number of liver enzymes that are above the reference ranges in the same individual (Fig. 2D). Aside from the proband sample, only one other sample had elevated bilirubin and PPCS levels (Table 1). 4 DISCUSSION Congenital disorders of glycosylation and Niemann-Pick disease, type C disease with infantile presentation are multisystemic diseases with overlapping clinical features including severe infantile-onset cholestatic liver disease. Timely and accurate diagnosis of these conditions with clinical, histologic, biochemical, and molecular evidence is imperative for early and appropriate treatment. The presentation of the index child provided the impetus for further evaluation of NPC-related markers in CDGs. False positive plasma oxysterol results may occur in the setting of acute liver disease, CDG, and other disorders.16 Results from this collection of samples suggest PPCS level in CDGs can be above the cut-off for NPC, and this may not necessarily relate to concurrent liver processes as transaminases, alkaline phosphatase, and bilirubin levels were not associated with elevations in PPCS. TCG is more",True,0,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
What biochemical markers are elevated in congenital disorders of glycosylation according to the study?,Elevated levels of oxysterol and N-palmitoyl-O-phosphocholineserine are observed in congenital disorders of glycosylation.,nihms-1872629.pdf,67,0,"Elevated Oxysterol and N-Palmitoyl- O-Phosphocholineserine Levels in Congenital Disorders of Glycosylation An N. Dang Do1,*,#, Irene J. Chang2,#, Xutian Jiang3, Lynne A. Wolfe4, Bobby G. Ng5, Christina Lam2,6, Rhonda E. Schnur7, Katrina Allis7, Hana Hansikova8, Nina Ondruskova8, Shawn D. O’Connor9, Amarilis Sanchez-Valle10, Arve Vollo11, Raymond Y. Wang12,13, Zoe Wolfenson4, John Perreault1, Daniel S. Ory3, Hudson H. Freeze5, J Lawrence Merritt2, Forbes D. Porter14 1Office of the Clinical Director, NICHD, NIH, Bethesda, MD, USA 2Division of Genetic Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA 3Department of Medicine, Washington University School of Medicine, Saint Louis, MO, USA 4Undiagnosed Diseases Program, Common Fund, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD, USA 5Human Genetics Program, Sanford Children’s Health Research Center, Sanford Burnham Prebys Medical Discovery Institute, La Jolla, CA, USA 6Center for Integrative Brain Research, Seattle Children’s Research Institute, Seattle, WA, USA 7GeneDx, Gaithersburg, MD, USA 8Department of Pediatrics and Inherited Metabolic Disorders, Charles University and General University Hospital in Prague, Prague, Czech Republic 9Department of Pediatrics, Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis, Saint Louis, MO, USA 10Division of Genetics and Metabolism, University of South Florida *Correspondence An Ngoc Dang Do, MD PhD, an.dangdo@nih.gov, 10 Center Drive, MSC 1103, Bethesda, MD 20892. Correspondence: An Ngoc Dang Do, MD PhD. An N. Dang Do is the guarantor for the article.#Contribute equally Authors Contributions: ADD, IJC, JLM, FDP conceptualized the study. ADD, IJC, XJ, LAW, BGN, CL, RES, KA, HH, NO, SDOC, ASV , A V , RYW, ZW,