Why are PPCS and TCG considered more specific biomarkers for Niemann-Pick disease type C (NPC) compared to plasma oxysterols?,"Plasma oxysterols such as 3β,5α,6β-triol and 7-ketocholesterol can be elevated in various conditions involving oxidative stress, including atherosclerosis, neurodegenerative diseases, liver dysfunction, and cholestatic liver disease, reducing their specificity for NPC. In contrast, PPCS and TCG have been explored as more specific markers for NPC, as they are less likely to be elevated in these other conditions.",nihms-1872629.pdf,67,2,"the traditional cell-based diagnostic methods. These metabolites include oxysterols [7-ketocholesterol (7KC) and cholestane- 3 β,5α,6β-triol (3β,5α,6β-triol)], N-palmitoyl- O-phosphocholineserine (PPCS), and a bile-acid derivative N-(3β,5α,6β-trihydroxy-cholan-24-oyl) glycine (TCG).6,7 N-acyl- O-phosphocholineserines belong to a relatively new lipid class characterized from biological samples, of which PPCS is the most abundant species.7 PPCS was previously mis-classified as lyso-sphingomyelin-like compound (lyso-SM509).8 Sterols (steroid alcohols) are biosynthetic compounds produced by plants (phytosterols) and animals (zoosterols such as cholesterol) that can be oxidized through non-enzymatic or enzymatic reactions to form oxysterols.9 Non-enzymatic oxidation of cholesterol produces the following oxysterols along various steps of the reaction pathways: 25-hydroxycholesterol (25HC), 7-KC, and 3 β,5α,6β-triol. The 3 β,5α,6β-triol is further converted to bile acids (e.g., TCG) from cholesterol precursors.6 While plasma oxysterols (3 β,5α,6β-triol and 7-KC) have been proposed to be sensitive and specific diagnostic biomarkers for NPC,10,11 these cholesterol oxidation products can be elevated in other conditions that result in oxidative stress, such as atherosclerosis, neurodegenerative diseases, and liver dysfunction.12 Specifically, plasma oxysterol levels can be elevated in individuals with cholestatic liver disease.13 Recent efforts have explored PPCS and TCG as more specific markers for NPC.14 We report a case of an infant presenting with neurologic and hepatic failure leading to early death, along with foamy macrophages on liver biopsy and elevated plasma oxysterols suggestive of NPC. Cholestasis gene panel and sequencing of NPC1 and NPC2 were negative, and the child was diagnosed post-mortem with ATP6AP1-CDG via exome sequencing. The elevated oxysterol levels in this index child prompted us to evaluate more NPC-specific markers, PPCS and TCG, in CDGs. 2. MATERIALS and METHODS 2.1 Clinical Case The study received exempt status by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Seattle Children’s Hospital. 2.2 Clinical Samples Samples were collected and analyzed on a clinical basis or per study protocols approved by the",True,3,gpt-4.1-mini,OpenAI Ada-002 (CPU/Cloud),biomedical-review
